    {
      "duration_ms": 8000,
      "caption": "Three modes control what goes through the journal: writeback, ordered (default), journal.",
      "narration": "A mount option picks what the journal protects: writeback, ordered, which is the default, or full data journaling.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Mount option data=",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 8,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "m",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "data=writeback",
            "data=ordered (default)",
            "data=journal"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 19.7,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "m",
          "at": 4500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 11000,
      "caption": "ordered: data reaches its final blocks before the metadata commit, so metadata never points at garbage.",
      "narration": "In ordered mode only metadata is journaled, but data blocks are forced out before the commit. Metadata never points at garbage, though in-place overwrites are not atomic.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "data=ordered",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 8,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "1. write data blocks",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 9,
          "color": "orange",
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "m",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "2. commit metadata txn",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 38,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 9,
          "color": "purple",
          "at": 2500,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "a1",
          "type": "arrow",
          "from": "d",
          "to": "m",
          "label": "before",
          "at": 2000,
          "enter": "draw"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In-place overwrites can be torn after a crash.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 56,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 10.8,
          "at": 5000,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "gray"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "journal: data and metadata both go through the journal: written twice, but atomic and consistent.",
      "narration": "In journal mode data goes through the journal too. It is written twice, so it is slower, but overwrites become atomic. It also disables delayed allocation.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "data=journal",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 8,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "j",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Data and metadata journaled",
            "Every block written twice",
            "Overwrites are atomic",
            "Delayed allocation disabled"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 25.6,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "j",
          "at": 6500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "writeback gives no data ordering; and renaming over a file still needs fsync for durability.",
      "narration": "Writeback mode can expose stale data from freed blocks after a crash. And even in ordered mode, replacing a file via rename needs fsync, unless auto_da_alloc guesses for you.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Pitfalls",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 8,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "p",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "writeback: stale data after crash",
            "ordered: write+rename still needs fsync",
            "auto_da_alloc: heuristic for replace-via-rename",
            "Atomic overwrite needs journal mode or app logic"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 43.2,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": "ordered keeps the filesystem consistent and metadata honest; only data=journal also makes data writes atomic, at double the write cost.",
